1
00:00:01,160 --> 00:00:02,200
One hundred sixty-one thousand dollars.

2
00:00:03,120 --> 00:00:06,980
That's what it costs to wait from
sixty-two to seventy for Social Security…

3
00:00:07,580 --> 00:00:10,600
paid out of your own savings, one month at
a time.

4
00:00:11,560 --> 00:00:14,900
And the check that wait buys you is bigger
for the rest of your life.

5
00:00:15,620 --> 00:00:20,230
Whether it was worth it depends on one
number nobody can look up: how long you

6
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:21,170
live.

7
00:00:21,220 --> 00:00:22,600
This is Run the Number.

8
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:27,420
By the end you'll know three things: what
each starting age does to the check,

9
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:32,050
the age where waiting starts to pay back,
and what the wait does to the savings

10
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:33,100
underneath it.

11
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:35,140
This is education, not advice.

12
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:39,000
The person in the example is an example,
not a real person.

13
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:40,840
The check first.

14
00:00:41,180 --> 00:00:43,570
Say your full benefit, the one Social
Security

15
00:00:43,620 --> 00:00:45,870
promises at sixty-seven, is twenty-four

16
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:47,100
hundred dollars a month.

17
00:00:47,560 --> 00:00:51,280
That's a little above the average retired
worker's check for twenty twenty-seven,

18
00:00:51,540 --> 00:00:54,340
which Social Security puts at about
twenty-one hundred seventy dollars.

19
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:58,820
Start at sixty-two, and the rules take
thirty percent off.

20
00:00:59,360 --> 00:01:00,360
For life.

21
00:01:00,780 --> 00:01:02,240
Sixteen hundred eighty dollars a month.

22
00:01:02,760 --> 00:01:05,060
Start at sixty-seven, you get the
twenty-four hundred.

23
00:01:05,760 --> 00:01:10,280
Wait to seventy, and every year past
sixty-seven adds eight percent.

24
00:01:10,330 --> 00:01:12,040
Twenty-four percent on top.

25
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:15,240
Two thousand nine hundred seventy-six
dollars a month.

26
00:01:15,880 --> 00:01:17,770
Three checks for the same work record.

27
00:01:17,820 --> 00:01:19,140
Sixteen eighty.

28
00:01:19,190 --> 00:01:20,520
Twenty-four hundred.

29
00:01:20,570 --> 00:01:21,940
Twenty-nine seventy-six.

30
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:25,860
The smallest and the largest are
seventy-seven percent apart…

31
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:28,240
and whichever one you pick, you keep.

32
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:33,110
The cost of living raise each January is a
percentage, so the bigger check gets

33
00:01:33,160 --> 00:01:35,840
the bigger raise, every year, forever.

34
00:01:38,300 --> 00:01:39,300
Now the wait.

35
00:01:39,880 --> 00:01:43,720
Waiting from sixty-two to sixty-seven
means five years with no check.

36
00:01:44,620 --> 00:01:46,230
Sixty months, times sixteen eighty.

37
00:01:46,280 --> 00:01:49,860
One hundred thousand eight hundred dollars
you didn't collect.

38
00:01:50,420 --> 00:01:54,920
In return, every check after sixty-seven
is seven hundred twenty dollars bigger.

39
00:01:55,660 --> 00:01:59,530
Divide what you gave up by what you gain,
and it takes one hundred forty months

40
00:01:59,580 --> 00:02:00,580
to get it back.

41
00:02:00,980 --> 00:02:02,560
Eleven years and eight months.

42
00:02:03,080 --> 00:02:05,080
You're even at seventy-eight and eight
months.

43
00:02:05,520 --> 00:02:07,970
Live past that, waiting won.

44
00:02:08,020 --> 00:02:09,740
Sixty-seven to seventy.

45
00:02:10,140 --> 00:02:13,800
Three years with no check: eighty-six
thousand four hundred dollars.

46
00:02:14,620 --> 00:02:17,100
The gain is five hundred seventy-six a
month.

47
00:02:17,620 --> 00:02:19,520
One hundred fifty months to break even.

48
00:02:19,940 --> 00:02:20,940
Eighty-two and a half.

49
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:24,140
And the full wait, sixty-two to seventy.

50
00:02:24,620 --> 00:02:25,620
Eight years.

51
00:02:26,380 --> 00:02:29,570
One hundred sixty-one thousand two hundred
eighty dollars of checks you didn't

52
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:32,360
take, for twelve hundred ninety-six more a
month.

53
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:34,380
Even at eighty and four months.

54
00:02:35,360 --> 00:02:36,480
Here's the honest part.

55
00:02:36,700 --> 00:02:41,350
Social Security's own table says a man
reaching sixty-five this year can expect

56
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:42,780
to live to about eighty-four.

57
00:02:43,460 --> 00:02:45,200
A woman, to about eighty-seven.

58
00:02:45,940 --> 00:02:48,960
Every break-even age on that list is under
both of those.

59
00:02:49,460 --> 00:02:52,180
So for the average person, waiting pays
back…

60
00:02:52,820 --> 00:02:53,820
by a few years.

61
00:02:54,360 --> 00:02:55,360
Not a landslide.

62
00:02:55,840 --> 00:02:58,040
Which is why the check isn't the whole
number.

63
00:02:59,400 --> 00:03:01,120
Here's the part that catches people.

64
00:03:01,780 --> 00:03:03,160
The wait isn't free.

65
00:03:03,900 --> 00:03:08,830
Somebody is paying your bills from
sixty-two to seventy, and that somebody is

66
00:03:08,880 --> 00:03:09,880
your savings.

67
00:03:10,340 --> 00:03:11,340
Run the number.

68
00:03:11,640 --> 00:03:14,060
Say you need four thousand dollars a month
to live on.

69
00:03:14,680 --> 00:03:17,720
Forty-eight thousand a year, before tax,
in today's dollars.

70
00:03:18,340 --> 00:03:19,340
Start at sixty-two.

71
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:22,040
The check covers sixteen eighty of it.

72
00:03:22,820 --> 00:03:24,500
Savings cover the other twenty-three
twenty.

73
00:03:24,980 --> 00:03:28,480
Over eight years, that's two hundred
twenty-two thousand seven hundred twenty

74
00:03:28,530 --> 00:03:30,220
dollars out of savings.

75
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:32,100
Wait to seventy.

76
00:03:32,760 --> 00:03:34,680
Savings cover the whole four thousand.

77
00:03:34,900 --> 00:03:35,900
Eight years…

78
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:38,080
three hundred eighty-four thousand
dollars.

79
00:03:38,860 --> 00:03:42,340
The difference is one hundred sixty-one
thousand two hundred eighty.

80
00:03:42,960 --> 00:03:45,380
Same number as before, from the other
side.

81
00:03:45,900 --> 00:03:46,900
That's the bridge.

82
00:03:47,460 --> 00:03:51,770
The person who started at sixty-two
arrives at seventy with a hundred
sixty-one

83
00:03:51,820 --> 00:03:53,730
thousand dollars more in the account…

84
00:03:53,780 --> 00:03:58,260
and a check that's twelve hundred
ninety-six a month smaller, for life.

85
00:03:59,040 --> 00:04:03,860
So the real question is which runs out
first: the extra savings, or you.

86
00:04:04,460 --> 00:04:08,810
If that money just sits, no growth above
inflation, twelve hundred ninety-six

87
00:04:08,860 --> 00:04:11,720
a month drains it in a hundred twenty-four
months.

88
00:04:12,300 --> 00:04:15,160
The early starter runs dry at eighty and
four months.

89
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:20,120
After that, the late starter is ahead
every single month, by twelve ninety-six.

90
00:04:21,020 --> 00:04:25,890
If it earns three percent a year above
inflation, cautious but not nothing, it

91
00:04:25,940 --> 00:04:27,720
lasts a hundred forty-nine months.

92
00:04:28,700 --> 00:04:29,700
Eighty-two and five months.

93
00:04:30,160 --> 00:04:33,740
At five percent above inflation,
eighty-four and eight months.

94
00:04:34,260 --> 00:04:36,500
Right about at the life expectancy table.

95
00:04:37,100 --> 00:04:38,700
Two more lines that move the number.

96
00:04:39,580 --> 00:04:40,190
One.

97
00:04:40,240 --> 00:04:42,920
If you're married, the bigger check
doesn't die with you.

98
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:47,610
When one spouse dies, the survivor keeps
the larger of the two checks and loses

99
00:04:47,660 --> 00:04:48,660
the smaller one.

100
00:04:49,090 --> 00:04:53,460
If the higher earner started at sixty-two,
the survivor's check is built on that

101
00:04:53,510 --> 00:04:54,510
reduced amount.

102
00:04:55,130 --> 00:04:58,950
Start at seventy, and the twenty-nine
seventy-six is what the survivor keeps.

103
00:04:59,590 --> 00:05:02,850
A couple is really running the number on
the longer of two lives.

104
00:05:03,790 --> 00:05:04,360
Two.

105
00:05:04,410 --> 00:05:08,170
If you start before sixty-seven and keep
working, there's an earnings limit.

106
00:05:08,670 --> 00:05:11,990
For twenty twenty-seven it's twenty-five
thousand five hundred sixty dollars.

107
00:05:13,150 --> 00:05:17,880
Above that, Social Security holds back one
dollar of benefits for every two you

108
00:05:17,930 --> 00:05:18,840
earn.

109
00:05:18,890 --> 00:05:23,540
It isn't lost for good, your check is
recalculated at full retirement age, but

110
00:05:23,590 --> 00:05:27,780
a working sixty-two-year-old can end up
with a reduced check and most of it held

111
00:05:27,830 --> 00:05:28,830
back.

112
00:05:28,970 --> 00:05:29,970
How to check yours.

113
00:05:30,650 --> 00:05:34,530
Log in at S S A dot gov and open your
Social Security Statement.

114
00:05:34,870 --> 00:05:39,420
The chart on page one shows your check at
every age from sixty-two to seventy,

115
00:05:39,470 --> 00:05:40,700
in today's dollars.

116
00:05:40,750 --> 00:05:46,120
Three numbers to write down: the one at
sixty-two, the one at sixty-seven, the

117
00:05:46,170 --> 00:05:47,170
one at seventy.

118
00:05:47,650 --> 00:05:50,150
Then your monthly spending, and what's in
savings.

119
00:05:50,670 --> 00:05:52,030
That's the whole calculation.

120
00:05:52,670 --> 00:05:57,780
A fee-only fiduciary or a C P A can run it
with your taxes and your spouse's record

121
00:05:57,830 --> 00:05:58,580
in it.

122
00:05:58,630 --> 00:05:59,630
Here's the pattern.

123
00:05:59,910 --> 00:06:02,790
The age on the application sets a check
you keep for life.

124
00:06:03,590 --> 00:06:07,430
Start early, and savings last longer but
the floor underneath you is lower.

125
00:06:07,930 --> 00:06:11,990
Start late, and the floor is higher but
the bridge comes out of savings first.

126
00:06:12,630 --> 00:06:15,730
The break-even ages are all under the life
expectancy table.

127
00:06:16,130 --> 00:06:19,510
The question the table can't answer is
which side of it you're on.

128
00:06:20,150 --> 00:06:21,210
One thing this week.

129
00:06:21,710 --> 00:06:22,710
Pull the statement.

130
00:06:22,950 --> 00:06:24,110
Write down the three numbers.

131
00:06:24,670 --> 00:06:26,870
Then write down what a month of your life
costs.

132
00:06:27,490 --> 00:06:29,600
If the gap between the sixty-two check and

133
00:06:29,650 --> 00:06:31,490
your spending is bigger than you expected,

134
00:06:31,930 --> 00:06:33,390
that's the number to sit with.

135
00:06:33,870 --> 00:06:35,790
New numbers every Tuesday and Friday.

136
00:06:36,710 --> 00:06:38,090
Subscribe so the next one finds you.

137
00:06:39,310 --> 00:06:41,390
Will your money last as long as you do?

